1 SET

   Changes a setting of DCL, the console, or the current process.

   Format

     SET keyword [value]

   The machine's own settings (breakpoints, stepping, the PSL, page
   table entries, the display modes) are the debugger's: type DEBUG,
   then HELP SET.  So are the console's string pool (SHOW STRING_POOL,
   CANCEL STRINGS) and the PSL's fields (HELP PSL).

2 ACL

   Changes a file's access control list: VMS's older form of SET
   SECURITY/ACL, which it shares.  HELP SET SECURITY has the entries'
   forms and what each qualifier does.

   Format

     SET ACL/ACL=(ace[,...]) file

3 Qualifiers
/ACL

      /ACL[=(ace[,...])]

   The entries to add, or with /DELETE or /REPLACE to delete or replace.

/AFTER

      /AFTER=(ace)

   Adds the entries after this one rather than first.

/DEFAULT

      /DEFAULT

   Gives the file the process's default protection and UIC, and deletes
   every entry but the protected ones.

/DELETE

      /DELETE[=ALL]

   Deletes /ACL's entries, or with none every entry but the protected
   ones; =ALL deletes every entry, then adds /ACL's.

/LIKE

      /LIKE=NAME=file

   Gives the file another file's protection and access control list.

/LOG

      /LOG
      /NOLOG (default)

   Reports each file changed.

/REPLACE

      /REPLACE=(ace[,...])

   Replaces each of /ACL's entries, in place, by these.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET ACL/ACL=(IDENTIFIER=SMITH,ACCESS=READ) LEDGER.DAT

     Lets SMITH read LEDGER.DAT.

2 CONTROL

   Turns Ctrl/Y on or off.  SET NOCONTROL=Y makes Ctrl/Y echo
   *Interrupt* and do nothing more, makes Ctrl/C do nothing to a program
   that hasn't enabled a Ctrl/C AST, and cancels the current command
   level's ON CONTROL_Y.  SET CONTROL=Y turns Ctrl/Y back on, with its
   default action.  The setting applies at every command level.  T
   (Ctrl/T) is accepted and remembered, as F$ENVIRONMENT("CONTROL")
   reports it, but govax shows no statistics for it.

   Format

     SET [NO]CONTROL[=(T,Y)]

   With neither T nor Y, Y is meant.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET NOCONTROL=Y

     Turns Ctrl/Y off.

2 DEBUG

   Turns govax's own debugging switches on or off (SET DBG is the same).
   SHOW DEBUG shows them.

   Format

     SET DEBUG [NO]name[,...]

   A name turns its switch on, and NOname off; SET DEBUG alone turns
   DEBUG on.  The names are:

     CHM          Debug change-mode instructions and REI.
     COMMAND      Show command lines after symbol substitution.
     DCL          Debug DCL's parsing of commands.
     DEBUG        Invoke the host's debugger, if there is one.
     DEVICES      Debug device operations.
     EXCEPTIONS   Debug exception handling.
     FULLDISASM   Show operand values in disassembly.
     IMAGES       Show an image's details on RUN.
     INTERRUPTS   Debug interrupt handling.
     KEYBOARD     Debug the console's keyboard input.
     LIBINIT      Call shareable images' LIB$INITIALIZE routines on RUN
                  (on by default; RUN/[NO]INIT overrides it).
     LOGICALS     Debug logical name operations.
     MEMORY       Debug memory operations.
     PROCESSES    Debug process operations (PROCESS is the same).
     PRIVILEGES   Show each privilege check: which process asked for
                  which operation, at which access mode, the privileges
                  the operation's rule needs, and whether it was granted.
                  Where a logical name table's protection can grant the
                  operation first, its answer is shown too, and whether
                  a privilege was then asked.
     PROTECTION   Show each access check: which UIC asked for what
                  access to which file, directory, mailbox, or global
                  section, its owner, protection code, and ACL, and
                  whether it was granted and by what.
     REGISTERS    Show changed registers after each STEP (on by
                  default).
     RMS          Show each RMS service's FAB or RAB as the service left
                  it, and its status by name.
     SERVICES     Debug system service calls through the P1 vector.
     SYMBOLS      Debug symbol table handling.
     TB           Debug translation buffer caching.
     USERHALT     HALT in user mode halts the CPU (on by default).
     USERSTEP     STEP steps only user-mode instructions (on by
                  default).
     VM           Debug virtual memory translations.

   P1 to P4 are accepted, and do nothing.

2 DEFAULT

   Sets the default device and directory, which every command's file
   names take a part from when they leave it out.

   Format

     SET DEFAULT [device:][directory]

   A part left out is the current default's: SET DEFAULT [MYDIR] keeps
   the device, and SET DEFAULT DUA1: the directory.  [-] is the
   directory above and [.SUB] one below.  Logical names are translated.
   The device goes into the logical name SYS$DISK, which is how every
   file command (and a program's $OPEN and $CREATE) finds the default
   device; a concealed logical name is kept as the device rather than
   what it hides.  A concealed rooted name is the device with its root,
   and [-] stops at the root, shown as [000000]; a root that isn't
   concealed is refused.  If the specification starts with a search list,
   SYS$DISK is set to the search list's name and each element is
   searched in turn.  SHOW DEFAULT shows the default.

   The default is always on a mounted volume.  A host path ("/tmp",
   "./src") is refused with VAX-E-HOSTDEF; name a host file by its own
   path ("./prog.mar") or with /HOST instead (see SPECIFY).

3 Examples

   1.$ SET DEFAULT DUA1:[MYDIR]

     Makes [MYDIR] on DUA1: the default directory.

   2.$ SET DEFAULT [-]

     Moves to the directory above.

2 EDITOR

   Says which host editor EDIT runs, and where its files go.  The values
   are the configuration keys vax.editor.command and vax.editor.scratch,
   saved when govax exits, so later sessions keep them.  SHOW EDITOR
   shows them.

   Format

     SET EDITOR

3 Qualifiers
/COMMAND

      /COMMAND="host command"

   The command to run.  %file in it stands for the file to edit; without
   one, the file goes last.  It is split into words at blanks, and quotes
   keep blanks in a word.  No shell runs it.  Quote the value, as DCL
   makes text without quotes uppercase.  The default is "vi %file"; ""
   goes back to it.

/SCRATCH

      /SCRATCH="host directory"

   An existing directory to work in.  EDIT makes a private directory in
   it for each file.  The default is the host's temporary directory; ""
   goes back to it.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET EDITOR/COMMAND="vim -n %file"

     Makes EDIT run vim.

   2.$ SET EDITOR/COMMAND="code --wait"/SCRATCH="/tmp"

     Makes EDIT run Visual Studio Code, working in /tmp.

2 FILE

   Changes a file's protection or owner: VMS's older forms of SET
   SECURITY/PROTECTION and /OWNER, which they share.  A list of files
   may be given, and each may have wildcards.  Changing a file's
   protection needs control access to it; giving it another owner needs
   privilege (HELP SET SECURITY).

   Format

     SET FILE/PROTECTION[=(code)] file[,...]
     SET FILE/OWNER_UIC[=uic] file[,...]

3 Qualifiers
/LOG

      /LOG
      /NOLOG (default)

   Reports each file changed, %SET-I-MODIFIED.

/OWNER_UIC

      /OWNER_UIC[=uic]

   Makes the UIC, or the identifier, the file's owner.  With no UIC, the
   current process's UIC.

/PROTECTION

      /PROTECTION[=(code)]

   Merges the code into the file's protection: a category the code
   doesn't name keeps what it had.  With no code, the file gets the
   process's default protection (SHOW PROTECTION).

3 Examples

   1.$ SET FILE/PROTECTION=(W:R) NOTES.TXT

     Lets the world read NOTES.TXT.

2 ON

   Turns a command procedure's checking of statuses on or off.  SET NOON
   makes the procedure go on whatever its commands' statuses: no failure
   ends it, and ON's command isn't run.  $STATUS and $SEVERITY are still
   set.  SET ON turns the checking back on, with the action that was in
   effect before.  Each command level has its own setting; at the
   terminal, SET [NO]ON does nothing.

   Format

     SET [NO]ON

2 PREFIX

   Puts a prefix before each verified command line (see SET VERIFY),
   made from a $FAO control string: the time each command runs, with
   "(!5%T) ".  The lines that continue a command get blanks as long as
   the prefix, and data lines get none.  The only directives that may
   take an argument are the time's and date's, !%T and !%D, which show
   the current time; a control string with another is refused
   (%DCL-E-INVPREFIX), and the prefix stays as it was.
   F$ENVIRONMENT("VERIFY_PREFIX") returns the control string; SET
   NOPREFIX takes it away.

   Format

     SET PREFIX "control-string"
     SET NOPREFIX

3 Examples

   1.$ SET PREFIX "(!5%T) "
     $ SET VERIFY
     $ @TEST
     (17:52) $ SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN

     Shows the time before each line TEST.COM runs.

2 PROCESS

   Changes a process: the current process's privileges, or the
   priority of a process, the current one or another, or suspends or
   resumes another process.

   A privilege the process is not authorized for (SHOW
   PROCESS/PRIVILEGES lists those it is) is not enabled, and the command
   says so with %SYSTEM-W-NOTALLPRIV, unless the process is authorized
   for SETPRV.  A change lasts until the next one, past each image's
   exit.  SYSTEM is authorized for every privilege, and holds them all,
   so every file protection check passes; turning off BYPASS, SYSPRV,
   GRPPRV, and READALL shows what a process without them would be
   refused, though SYSTEM's UIC, [1,4], keeps it in the SYSTEM category
   of every protection code.

   Another process is changed as $SETPRI, $SUSPND, and $RESUME would
   change it: one with the process's own UIC freely, one in its UIC
   group with GROUP or WORLD, any other with WORLD.  A refusal is

     %SET-E-NOTSET, error modifying 00000302
     -SYSTEM-F-NOPRIV, insufficient privilege or object protection violation

   Format

     SET PROCESS [process-name]

3 Parameter

   process-name

   The name of the process to change, looked up among the processes of
   the current process's UIC group.  /IDENTIFICATION names a process by
   its PID instead.  With neither, the current process is changed.

3 Qualifiers
/IDENTIFICATION

      /IDENTIFICATION=pid

   The process to change, by its PID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
   shows it.  It overrides the process-name parameter.

/PRIORITY

      /PRIORITY=n

   The process's base priority, 0 to 31.  A priority above the current
   process's own base priority needs ALTPRI:

     %SET-E-NOTSET, need ALTPRI privilege to elevate above base priority

/PRIVILEGES

      /PRIVILEGES=(privilege[,...])

   The privileges to enable, each by its name (BYPASS, SYSPRV, ...), and
   to disable, each with NO before its name (NOBYPASS).  ALL is every
   privilege, NOALL none.  The parentheses may be left out for one.
   Only the current process's privileges are changed.

/RESUME

   Lets a suspended process run again.

/SUSPEND

   Stops a process from running until SET PROCESS/RESUME (or $RESUME)
   lets it go on.  The current process isn't suspended.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET PROCESS/PRIVILEGES=(NOREADALL,NOGRPPRV,NOSYSPRV,NOBYPASS)

     Turns off the four privileges that bear on file protection.

   2.$ SET PROCESS/PRIORITY=3/IDENTIFICATION=00000302

     Lowers process 00000302's base priority to 3.

2 PROMPT

   Changes the prompt the console shows when it reads a command.  The
   new prompt is also stored as the configuration value
   vax.console.prompt, so it stays in effect in later sessions.  The
   assembler's ASM> and the debugger's DBG> prompts don't change.

   Format

     SET PROMPT="text"

   Quote the text, so that its case and any blanks (such as a trailing
   one) are kept.  An empty string ("") returns to the default prompt,
   "VAX> ".

3 Examples

   1.$ SET PROMPT="vax$ "

     Makes the prompt vax$.

2 PROTECTION

   With /DEFAULT, changes the current process's default file
   protection: the protection a new file gets when nothing else gives it
   one.  The code is merged into the default: a category it doesn't name
   keeps what it had, and with no code nothing changes.  A process
   starts with the default the configuration's vax.sysgen.rms_fileprot
   gives, normally (S:RWED,O:RWED,G:RE,W), and a process it creates gets
   its own.  SHOW PROTECTION shows it.

   A protection code is a list of categories, SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP, and
   WORLD (or their first letters), each with the access it gives: R
   (read), W (write), E (execute), D (delete).  ":" or "=" may follow a
   category, and a category with no letters gives no access.

   Without /DEFAULT, SET PROTECTION[=(code)] file changes a file's
   protection, VMS's older form of SET SECURITY/PROTECTION: the code is
   merged into the file's, and with no code the file gets the process's
   default protection.  It needs control access to the file.

   Format

     SET PROTECTION[=(code)]/DEFAULT
     SET PROTECTION[=(code)] file

3 Qualifiers
/DEFAULT

      /DEFAULT

   Changes the process's default protection.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET PROTECTION=(G:RWED,W:R)/DEFAULT
     $ SHOW PROTECTION
       SYSTEM=RWED, OWNER=RWED, GROUP=RWED, WORLD=R

     Gives the group every access and the world read access to the
     files made from now on.

2 QUANTUM

   Sets how many instructions run between the points where an interrupt
   or a process switch can happen.  0 suspends interrupt delivery
   (%VAX-I-NOINTERRUPTS), and a later value above 0 resumes it.  Change
   it only to debug interrupts and process switching.  The default comes
   from the vax.quantum setting.

   Format

     SET QUANTUM count

2 ENTRY

   Changes a batch job that isn't executing (%JBC-E-EXECUTING): holds or
   releases it, gives it another time, or changes its options.  A job
   you didn't submit needs delete access to it by an ACE, or manage
   access to its queue (%JBC-E-NOPRIV).

   Format

     SET ENTRY entry[,...]

3 Parameter

 entry

   The entry numbers of the jobs to change.

3 Qualifiers
/AFTER

      /[NO]AFTER=time

   Holds the job until the time, as SUBMIT/AFTER; /NOAFTER makes it
   pending now.

/CPUTIME

      /CPUTIME=limit

   The job's CPU time limit, as SUBMIT/CPUTIME.

/HOLD

   Holds the job until SET ENTRY/RELEASE.

/LOG_FILE

      /LOG_FILE=file-spec

   The job's log file, as SUBMIT/LOG_FILE.

/NAME

      /NAME=job-name

   The job's name.

/NOTE

      /NOTE="text"

   The job's note.

/PARAMETERS

      /PARAMETERS=(p1[,...])

   The job's parameters, all of them replaced.

/PRIORITY

      /PRIORITY=n

   The job's place in its queue, as SUBMIT/PRIORITY.

/RELEASE

   Releases a held job: it is pending, whatever its /AFTER time.

/REQUEUE

      /REQUEUE=queue-name

   Moves the job to another queue.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET ENTRY 19/RELEASE

     Releases job 19.

2 QUEUE

   Changes a batch queue's attributes, in any state.  It takes
   INITIALIZE/QUEUE's attributes, and needs manage access to the queue
   (%JBC-E-NOPRIV).

   Format

     SET QUEUE queue-name

3 Parameter

 queue-name

   The queue to change.

3 Qualifiers
/BASE_PRIORITY

      /BASE_PRIORITY=n

   The base priority, 0 to 31, of the queue's jobs' processes.  The
   default is 4.

/CPUDEFAULT

      /[NO]CPUDEFAULT=hh:mm:ss

   The CPU time limit a job gets when it gives none.  INFINITE is no
   limit.

/CPUMAXIMUM

      /[NO]CPUMAXIMUM=hh:mm:ss

   The most CPU time a job may ask for.

/DESCRIPTION

      /[NO]DESCRIPTION="text"

   A line of text describing the queue, which SHOW QUEUE shows.

/JOB_LIMIT

      /JOB_LIMIT=n

   How many jobs the queue runs at once, 1 to 255.  The default is 1.

/OWNER_UIC

      /OWNER_UIC=uic

   The queue's owner, a UIC or an identifier.  The default is [SYSTEM].

/PROTECTION

      /PROTECTION=(category:access[,...])

   The queue's protection code.  A queue's kinds of access are R (read:
   see its jobs), S (submit), M (manage), and D (delete: change or
   delete one's own jobs).  The default is (S:M,O:D,G:R,W:S).  SET
   SECURITY/CLASS=QUEUE changes it too, and the queue's ACL.

/RETAIN

      /[NO]RETAIN[=option]

   Which ended jobs the queue keeps: ALL (the default option) or ERROR,
   those that didn't succeed.  /NORETAIN keeps none.

/WSDEFAULT

      /[NO]WSDEFAULT=n

   The queue's working-set default for its jobs, in pages.  govax keeps
   and shows the working-set values; a job's process has its account's.

/WSEXTENT

      /[NO]WSEXTENT=n

   The queue's working-set extent for its jobs, in pages.

/WSQUOTA

      /[NO]WSQUOTA=n

   The queue's working-set quota for its jobs, in pages.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET QUEUE/JOB_LIMIT=4/DESCRIPTION="Nightly work" SYS$BATCH

     Lets SYS$BATCH run four jobs at once, and describes it.

2 RADIX

   Sets the console's radix: the base of numbers PRINT and the console's
   expressions read and write without a radix prefix (X^, D^, O^).  SHOW
   RADIX shows it.  The debugger has a radix of its own.

   Format

     SET RADIX radix

   The radix is HEXADECIMAL, DECIMAL, or OCTAL, abbreviated to no less
   than three letters, or 16, 10, or 8.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL
     $ PRINT 10+10

     Prints 00000020.

2 SECURITY

   Changes a file's security profile: its owner, its protection code,
   and its access control list (ACL).  The file specification may have
   wildcards (a name with no version is its highest version), but only
   one may be given.  Changing a profile needs control access to the
   file: the SYSTEM and OWNER categories have it, and so does anyone an
   ACL entry gives CONTROL.  Giving a file another owner needs more: a
   system UIC (a group up to vax.sysgen.maxsysgroup), SYSPRV, or BYPASS,
   or GRPPRV for an owner in its own group, though anyone with control
   may make their own UIC the owner again.

   A protection code is a list of categories, SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP, and
   WORLD (or their first letters), each with the access it gives: R
   (read), W (write), E (execute), D (delete); HELP SET PROTECTION.

   An ACL entry (ACE) is a list in parentheses:

     (IDENTIFIER=id[+id...][,OPTIONS=options][,ACCESS=access])
     (DEFAULT_PROTECTION[,OPTIONS=options],code)
     (CREATOR[,OPTIONS=options],ACCESS=access)
     (ALARM=SECURITY[,OPTIONS=options],ACCESS=access+SUCCESS+FAILURE)
     (AUDIT=SECURITY[,OPTIONS=options],ACCESS=access+SUCCESS+FAILURE)

   An identifier is a UIC ([200,1], [200,*], [SMITH]), a general
   identifier, or an environmental one (INTERACTIVE); access is NONE or
   READ, WRITE, EXECUTE, DELETE, and CONTROL joined by "+"; options are
   DEFAULT, PROTECTED, NOPROPAGATE, and NONE.  The first identifier ACE
   that matches a process decides what it may do.  A directory's
   DEFAULT ACEs go to the files made in it, and its default protection
   ACE gives them their protection.  Alarms and audits are kept and
   shown, and do nothing.  HIDDEN entries can't be changed here.

   Format

     SET SECURITY[/qualifiers] file

3 Qualifiers
/ACL

      /ACL[=(ace[,...])]

   Adds the entries, in order, before the first entry (or after /AFTER's
   entry).  An entry for the same identifiers as one already there takes
   its place.  With /DELETE or /REPLACE, the entries to delete or to
   replace.

/AFTER

      /AFTER=(ace)

   Adds /ACL's entries after this one.

/BY_OWNER

      /BY_OWNER=uic

   Changes only the files this UIC owns; "*" may stand for the group or
   the member.

/CLASS

      /CLASS=class

   The class of object: FILE, the default, or QUEUE, a batch queue's
   profile.  A queue's protection code gives R (read: see the queue's
   security and jobs), S (submit), M (manage: start, stop, change, or
   delete the queue, and do anything to its jobs; M implies R, S, and
   D), and D (delete: change or delete a job).  The code's R and D reach
   only the user's own jobs; an ACE's reach every job in the queue.  An
   ACE's access words are READ, SUBMIT, MANAGE, DELETE, and CONTROL.
   Changing a queue's profile needs control access (its owner, the
   SYSTEM category, an ACE's CONTROL, BYPASS) or OPER; a refusal is
   %SYSTEM-F-NOPRIV.  /DEFAULT gives the queue the class's template,
   [SYSTEM] and (S:M,O:D,G:R,W:S), with no ACL; /LIKE, /EXCLUDE, and
   /BY_OWNER are for files.

     $ SET SECURITY/CLASS=QUEUE -
       /ACL=(IDENTIFIER=PAYROLL,ACCESS=READ+SUBMIT) SYS$BATCH

/COPY_ATTRIBUTE

      /COPY_ATTRIBUTE=OWNER

   With /LIKE, copies the other file's owner too.

/DEFAULT

      /DEFAULT

   Gives the file the process's default protection (a directory, its
   parent's less delete) and UIC, and deletes every ACL entry but the
   protected ones.

/DELETE

      /DELETE[=ALL]

   Deletes /ACL's entries (one that isn't there is %SET-W-NOSUCHACE), or
   with none every entry but the protected ones; =ALL deletes every
   entry, then adds /ACL's.

/EXCLUDE

      /EXCLUDE=(file[,...])

   Leaves out the files these names match.

/LIKE

      /LIKE=NAME=file

   Gives the file another file's protection and ACL (but its NOPROPAGATE
   entries), keeping the file's own protected entries first.

/LOG

      /LOG
      /NOLOG (default)

   Reports each file changed, %SET-I-MODIFIED.

/OWNER

      /OWNER=uic

   Makes the UIC or identifier the file's owner: [200,1], [SMITH],
   SMITH, or a general identifier.

/PROTECTION

      /PROTECTION=(code)

   Merges the code into the file's protection: a category the code
   doesn't name keeps what it had.

/REPLACE

      /REPLACE=(ace[,...])

   Replaces each of /ACL's entries, in place, by these.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET SECURITY/PROTECTION=(G:RWED,W) LEDGER.DAT

     Gives the owner's group every access and the world none.

   2.$ SET SECURITY/ACL=(IDENTIFIER=[200,*],ACCESS=READ+WRITE) LEDGER.DAT

     Lets everyone in group 200 read and write LEDGER.DAT, whatever its
     protection code says.

   3.$ SET SECURITY/OWNER=[200,1]/LOG *.DAT

     Gives every .DAT file in the default directory to [200,1].

2 TERMINAL

   Changes a terminal's characteristics: those its qualifiers name, the
   rest left as they are.  govax's terminal is its console, TTA0:, and
   its characteristics are what programs read (IO$_SENSEMODE, F$GETDVI,
   SHOW DEVICES/FULL); changing them changes nothing about the host's
   terminal window.

   Each characteristic's qualifier sets one bit of the terminal's
   characteristics, the TT$M_ or TT2$M_ bit its description names, and
   with NO clears it.

   Format

     SET TERMINAL [device-name[:]]

3 Parameter

   device-name[:]

   The terminal.  The default is SYS$COMMAND's.  A device that isn't a
   terminal is refused with %SET-W-NOTSET.

3 Qualifiers
/ADVANCED_VIDEO

      /[NO]ADVANCED_VIDEO

   The terminal has the advanced video option (TT2$M_AVO).

/ALTYPEAHD

      /[NO]ALTYPEAHD

   The terminal's type-ahead buffer is the alternate, larger one
   (TT2$M_ALTYPEAHD).

/ANSI_CRT

      /[NO]ANSI_CRT

   The terminal follows the ANSI standard for video terminals
   (TT2$M_ANSICRT).

/APPLICATION_KEYPAD

      /[NO]APPLICATION_KEYPAD

   The keypad sends application sequences rather than digits
   (TT2$M_APP_KEYPAD).

/BLOCK_MODE

      /[NO]BLOCK_MODE

   The terminal can send a screen at a time (TT2$M_BLOCK).

/BRDCSTMBX

      /[NO]BRDCSTMBX

   Broadcast messages also go to the terminal's associated mailbox
   (TT2$M_BRDCSTMBX).

/BROADCAST

      /[NO]BROADCAST

   The terminal takes broadcast messages ($BRKTHRU's); /NOBROADCAST sets
   TT$M_NOBRDCST.

/DEC_CRT

      /[NO]DEC_CRT

   The terminal is a DIGITAL video terminal (TT2$M_DECCRT).

/DEC_CRT2

      /[NO]DEC_CRT2

   The terminal is a DIGITAL VT200-level terminal (TT2$M_DECCRT2).

/DEVICE_TYPE

      /DEVICE_TYPE=type

   The terminal type, by its name in $TTDEF without TT$_: VT52, VT100,
   VT200_SERIES, LA120, and the rest.  Only the type is set; the
   characteristics that go with it on VMS are left as they are.

/DISCONNECT

      /[NO]DISCONNECT

   A process stays when its line hangs up, for a later reconnection
   (TT2$M_DISCONNECT).

/DMA

      /[NO]DMA

   The terminal's controller transfers by DMA (TT2$M_DMA).

/ECHO

      /[NO]ECHO

   The terminal driver echoes what is typed; /NOECHO sets TT$M_NOECHO.

/EDIT_MODE

      /[NO]EDIT_MODE

   The terminal has the ANSI editing functions (TT2$M_EDIT).

/EIGHT_BIT

      /[NO]EIGHT_BIT

   The terminal uses 8-bit characters (TT$M_EIGHTBIT).

/ESCAPE

      /[NO]ESCAPE

   Escape sequences typed are validated as such (TT$M_ESCAPE).

/FORM

      /[NO]FORM

   The terminal has mechanical form feeds (TT$M_MECHFORM).

/HANGUP

      /[NO]HANGUP

   The line hangs up when its process logs out (TT2$M_HANGUP).

/HARDCOPY

      /HARDCOPY

   The terminal is a printing one: the opposite of /SCOPE.  It takes no
   NO.

/HOSTSYNC

      /[NO]HOSTSYNC

   The system stops the terminal's input with XOFF when it can't keep up
   (TT$M_HOSTSYNC).

/INSERT

      /[NO]INSERT

   Line editing starts in insert mode (TT2$M_INSERT).

/LINE_EDITING

      /[NO]LINE_EDITING

   The terminal driver's line editing is on (TT2$M_EDITING).

/LOCAL_ECHO

      /[NO]LOCAL_ECHO

   The terminal echoes what is typed itself (TT2$M_LOCALECHO).

/LOWERCASE

      /[NO]LOWERCASE

   The terminal has lowercase letters (TT$M_LOWER); /NOLOWERCASE is
   /UPPERCASE.

/MODEM

      /[NO]MODEM

   The terminal is on a modem line (TT$M_MODEM).

/PAGE

      /PAGE=n

   The page length, from 0 to 255 lines.

/PASTHRU

      /[NO]PASTHRU

   Everything typed is passed to the program unread, but XON and XOFF
   (TT2$M_PASTHRU).

/PERMANENT

      /[NO]PERMANENT

   Changes the terminal's permanent characteristics, those it has when a
   process logs in on it, as well as its current ones.  This needs
   LOG_IO or PHY_IO; without one, the command changes nothing and says

     %SET-W-NOTSET, error modifying TTA0:
     -SYSTEM-F-NOPRIV, insufficient privilege or object protection violation

   govax keeps one set of characteristics for a terminal, so the
   permanent and current ones are the same.

/PRINTER_PORT

      /[NO]PRINTER_PORT

   The terminal has a printer port (TT2$M_PRINTER).

/READSYNC

      /[NO]READSYNC

   The system asks the terminal for input with XON and stops it with
   XOFF (TT$M_READSYNC).

/REGIS

      /[NO]REGIS

   The terminal understands ReGIS graphics (TT2$M_REGIS).

/SCOPE

      /[NO]SCOPE

   The terminal is a video one (TT$M_SCOPE).

/SIXEL_GRAPHICS

      /[NO]SIXEL_GRAPHICS

   The terminal understands sixel graphics (TT2$M_SIXEL).

/TAB

      /[NO]TAB

   The terminal has mechanical tabs (TT$M_MECHTAB).

/TTSYNC

      /[NO]TTSYNC

   The terminal stops output with XOFF and starts it with XON
   (TT$M_TTSYNC).

/TYPE_AHEAD

      /[NO]TYPE_AHEAD

   What is typed before a program reads is kept; /NOTYPE_AHEAD sets
   TT$M_NOTYPEAHD.

/UPPERCASE

      /UPPERCASE

   The terminal has no lowercase letters: the opposite of /LOWERCASE.
   It takes no NO.

/WIDTH

      /WIDTH=n

   The page width, from 1 to 511 columns.

/WRAP

      /[NO]WRAP

   A line longer than the width goes on to the next line (TT$M_WRAP).

3 Examples

   1.$ SET TERMINAL/WIDTH=132/PAGE=48

     A wide screen of 48 lines.

   2.$ SET PROCESS/PRIVILEGES=LOG_IO
     $ SET TERMINAL/PERMANENT/NOBROADCAST

     Turns broadcast messages off for good, which needs LOG_IO.

2 VERBOSE

   Turns the console's verbose messages on or off: PRINT's output, and
   the message when a HALT stops the CPU.  It is on unless SET NOVERBOSE
   has turned it off.

   Format

     SET [NO]VERBOSE

2 VERIFY

   Shows each line of a command procedure (@) as it runs: procedure
   verification shows its command lines, and image verification its
   data lines, as an image (or TYPE SYS$INPUT), the debugger, or the
   assembler reads each one.

   Format

     SET VERIFY[=([NO]PROCEDURE,[NO]IMAGE)]
     SET NOVERIFY

   SET VERIFY and SET NOVERIFY turn both on or off; a keyword, which may
   be abbreviated, changes only its own.  A change lasts after the
   procedure that made it ends.

   A command line is shown as it is in the file, its "$", indentation,
   label, and comment included, after 'SYMBOL' substitution (&SYMBOL and
   the symbols of an expression are not shown replaced).  Comment lines
   are shown too, and a continued command a line at a time.  The lines
   of an IF branch that doesn't run are not shown (its ELSE or ENDIF is),
   nor is the THEN of an IF that was false, an ON command's action, or a
   command typed at the terminal.

   F$VERIFY reads and sets both settings, and DCL performs it even in a
   comment, between apostrophes, showing the comment with its value in
   place of the call: a procedure that starts with

     $ ! 'F$VERIFY(0)'

   turns verification off without that line being shown.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET VERIFY=(NOPROCEDURE,IMAGE)

     Shows data lines, but not command lines.

2 WORKING_SET

   Changes the current process's working set values, within the quota
   and extent it was authorized when it was created (SHOW WORKING_SET
   shows them).  A value above its authorized bound is set to the bound,
   and the command then reports the values, as /LOG does.  The current
   limit is set to the new default at once (DCL runs between images).
   With paging on (vax.sysgen.paging.enable), a smaller limit takes the
   oldest pages out of the working set.

   Format

     SET WORKING_SET

3 Qualifiers
/ADJUST

      /[NO]ADJUST

   Whether the system may adjust the working set by itself.  govax
   remembers and shows the setting, but has no automatic adjustment.

/EXTENT

      /EXTENT=n

   The working set extent: the most $ADJWSL can reach.  At most the
   authorized extent, and more than the SYSGEN parameter MINWSCNT.  A
   smaller extent brings the quota and limit down with it.

/LIMIT

      /LIMIT=n

   The default working set, in pages: the limit each image starts with,
   and goes back to at its exit.  A value above the quota raises the
   quota too.

/LOG

      /[NO]LOG

   Reports the new values.  /NOLOG is the default.

/QUOTA

      /QUOTA=n

   The working set quota: the most an image's $ADJWSL can count on.  At
   most the authorized quota and the extent.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET WORKING_SET/QUOTA=5000
     %SET-I-NEWLIMS, new working set:  Limit = 512  Quota = 1024  Extent = 16400

     Asks for a larger quota than authorized, and gets the authorized
     one.
